Z||000000||fldevel||12-09-2001||01:52 PM||||argh ..ok origonaly i hit a squirrel and it cracked the tank in the radiator so i took the radiator out of my 2m4 and stuck it in there but noticed the origonal owner had stripped the auto trans cooler hole out on the bottom and jimmi rigged it .. so i took his jimmi rigging off that car and stuck it on my car for a few days so i could drive it but the trans lines where leaking bad ..so i finaly got a new tank put on the origonal radiator and put it in my car...and now it will idle for a few and than all the pressure will build up in the raidiator and will just overheat ..ive burped the system and done everything everyone has told me to do but no luck .. i really need a car soon .. i just pulled apart my GT to put a 5 speed in it and now this happens .. its just my luck .. ||||reg||8
Z||000001||fierospeeder||12-09-2001||02:46 PM||||are you saying, that your taking a 2m4 radiator and putting it on a 2m6?<P>im not sure if the radiators are different.  Someone else might know, but usually, a bigger engine will create more heat.<P>Plus, if you did swap the radiators, it could have been clogged.||||reg||1
Z||000002||K2m4||12-09-2001||02:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fldevel:<BR><B>argh ..ok origonaly i hit a squirrel and it cracked the tank in the radiator so i took the radiator out of my 2m4 and stuck it in there but noticed the origonal owner had stripped the auto trans cooler hole out on the bottom and jimmi rigged it .. so i took his jimmi rigging off that car and stuck it on my car for a few days so i could drive it but the trans lines where leaking bad ..so i finaly got a new tank put on the origonal radiator and put it in my car...and now it will idle for a few and than all the pressure will build up in the raidiator and will just overheat ..ive burped the system and done everything everyone has told me to do but no luck .. i really need a car soon .. i just pulled apart my GT to put a 5 speed in it and now this happens .. its just my luck .. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Sounds like you still have air in the system...follow ogres proceedures in the cave and reburp the system<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||12-09-2001||05:34 PM||||Make sure you jack the rear of the car as high as possible before you burp it. Remove the thermostat cap and take out the thermostat. Open the radiator cap, you should see fluid pour out of the radiator. If you are seeing this you can cap it again. If not fill the car from the thermostat housing till you see/hear coolant flowing out of the radiator. Top of the overflow reservoir to the cold fill line. Finish filling the system from the rear of the car. Lower the car run for a minute and recheck the level at the rear of the car. The thermostat housing is higher than the radiator and if the system is full you should see coolant above the coolant hose coming off the thermostat housing. Mine is right up to the cap. Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fldevel||12-10-2001||11:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierospeeder:<BR><B>are you saying, that your taking a 2m4 radiator and putting it on a 2m6?<P>im not sure if the radiators are different.  Someone else might know, but usually, a bigger engine will create more heat.<P>Plus, if you did swap the radiators, it could have been clogged.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>actualy the radiators i think are the same ..the only diff is the autos have a cooling core for the trans and the manuals dont ..the raidiator from the 2m4 worked fine for the week that it was in the car but after i put the origonal back in is when it started doing this .. also i have the car sitting on a rather steep hill when i re-fill it so i dont think i need to jack the car up and im rather sure all the air is out .. i was thinking it might be one of those plastic impeller watter pumps and when i did overheat it might have damaged it a bit??? but on an 88 formula those things are a pain to get off  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||12-10-2001||03:52 PM||||It could also be a stuck thermostat.  Very easy to check with a pan of water on the stove.  Also cheap and easy to replace.<BR>The plastic impeller may also be a concern.  I just replaced the pump on my 88 GT with a metal impeller one.  It's not too bad a job, although I'm not a real fan of Torx bolts.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
